  One caller, at [38;5;153mapp.js:17-27[39m, inside the form submit handler. It reads [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m out of the DOM. There is
  no user ID anywhere in the form or the page.

  [1mThe fork:[0m adding the parameter is trivial; the real question is where the caller gets a value to pass. That decision
  is the one you have to live with, because it determines what "who logged in" actually identifies and whether it's
  stable across sessions.

  - [1mCaller-supplied from a new form/page source[0m — you add a hidden input or read it from a data attribute the server
    rendered. Buys a real, server-meaningful ID. Costs a change to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and an assumption that something upstream
    knows the ID. Rules out nothing later.
  - [1mClient-generated ID (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m, persisted in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — buys a stable per-browser identifier
    with zero server coordination. Costs: it identifies a browser, not a person, so it will not match any user record
    you have. Hard to change later once IDs are in logs.
  - [1mDerive it from [38;5;153musername[0m — buys nothing you don't already have; [38;5;153mlogin[39m already logs and returns the username.
    Cheapest, but the parameter would be redundant.

  [1mMy recommendation: caller-supplied.[0m A tracking ID that can't be joined to a real user record is tracking you'll have
  to redo, and the other two options give you exactly that. The cost here is one hidden input in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.

  Two smaller points I'll fold into whichever you pick: I'd make [38;5;153muserId[39m the [1mfirst[0m parameter (identity before
  credentials) or the last — and I'd keep it [1moptional[0m so the single existing call site can't silently break if the ID is
  absent.
